// GC index-table leak regression probe (#2756, #2922, #2978). Live set of at
// most two objects per iteration -- the shape that drove
// runtime/gc/gcindex.bit's idxGrow to abandon the whole rehashed
// address->object index table into the bump-only gcIdxRes reserve after
// every sweep, never reusing it. See _tests_/bit/benchgate.bit's baseline
// comment for the fixed/broken RSS this case guards.
//
// WHY THE RUNNING SUM LIVES IN THE OBJECT (#3667). This loop used to keep its
// accumulator entirely in a local and never read the objects back, so nothing
// it allocated escaped: #3622's dead-allocation elimination proved all
// 4,000,000 of them unobservable and deleted the whole loop, taking the case
// to 7 objects and `idxchunks` to 0 while printing the identical answer. A
// leak probe that allocates nothing tests nothing. So each iteration now
// stores the running sum INTO its object and retains that object across the
// loop back edge, and the print reads the last one: the allocations are load-
// bearing for the printed answer, so no correct optimizer can remove them.
// Do NOT "simplify" this back into a local-only accumulator, and do not
// exempt this file from optimization to keep the count up -- the benchmark
// must allocate for real under full optimization.
//
// `sink` stays a plain local so the loop's arithmetic remains register-only
// and the per-iteration cost is what it always was: the object is written,
// not read, until the loop ends. Only the retain and the final read are new.
class Big { a: i64, b: i64 }

fn main() {
  let n = 0
  let sink = 0
  let acc = Big{ a: 0, b: 0 }
  while (n < 4000000) {
    sink = sink + n
    acc = Big{ a: n, b: sink }
    n = n + 1
  }
  print("${acc.b}\n")
}
